<p>This page helps property owners determine if SR-177 affects their property and provides step-by-step guidance on what to do at each phase of the project.</p>

<div class="callout info">
  <p><strong>Key dates (as of November 10, 2026):</strong> UDOT published the Draft State Environmental Study on <strong>November 6, 2026</strong>. It identifies a Preferred Alternative, and Volume 2 maps the proposed right-of-way parcel by parcel.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol2-nov2026" %} Comments are due <strong>December 11, 2026</strong>: by email to sr177extension@utah.gov, through the online comment form linked from the study page, by mail to the SR-177 Study Team, 1265 E Fort Union Blvd, Suite 200, Cottonwood Heights, UT 84047, or in person at the hearing.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %} The official in-person public hearing is set for <strong>Tuesday, November 10, 4:30–7:30 p.m.</strong> at Roy Junior High School, 5347 South 2100 West, Roy, with right-of-way specialists available for property-specific questions; the November 9 online presentation recording is to be posted beginning November 11.{% cite "udot-ses-public-hearing-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-ses-online-presentation-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %} Invitation-only neighborhood meetings for affected owners and tenants were held in early November.{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %}</p>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<div class="section-label jump-target" id="geographic-screening">Step 1: Determine If Your Property Is Affected</div>
<h2>Geographic Screening</h2>

<p><strong>The SR-177 corridor will primarily affect properties based on proximity to the planned alignment.</strong> UDOT's Draft State Environmental Study, published November 6, 2026, is now the best source for a specific parcel: Volume 2 contains a Preferred Alternative and Environmental Resources mapbook and a Right-of-Way Impacts mapbook that shows proposed relocations and partial acquisitions.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol2-nov2026" %} The draft estimates 73 to 77 residential relocations (it gives both figures), three commercial relocations, and partial acquisitions from about 116 to 120 parcels.{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %} The general impact zones are:</p>

<div class="callout warning">
  <p><strong>The draft maps are not an acquisition notice:</strong> UDOT says property impacts "may be refined during the final design and right-of-way acquisition phases," and the alignment is not final until the Final State Environmental Study is approved (expected early 2027).{% cite "udot-draft-ses-vol1-nov2026" %}{% cite "udot-ses-schedule" %} Public ArcGIS working layers are not a substitute for the published mapbooks.{% cite "arcgis-sr177-row-service-may2026" %} If your parcel is shown, confirm it with UDOT's right-of-way staff before making decisions.</p>
</div>

